    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One specific issue is that the clause in this draft is quite vague—it just mentions \"unforeseen circumstances beyond the control of the parties\" without listing specific examples like government mandates or law changes. Given that ambiguity, how much weight do courts usually give to the \"unforeseen\" nature of the interruption when deciding if the rent obligation should be suspended?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Singaporan courts apply a strict \"literal interpretation\" to force majeure clauses, meaning the tenant must prove the regulatory change was unforeseeable at the time the lease was signed for relief to apply. If the vagueness allows the court to interpret the term broadly, the tenant may still claim relief only if they can demonstrate the event was truly unexpected and not a reasonable consequence of known market conditions or ongoing government reforms. Consequently, without explicit language gating \"statutory changes\" or \"administrative orders,\" ambiguity will likely disadvantage the tenant and leave the default rent obligation intact.",
      "source": "target",
